# kevinpierik.dev

`kevinpierik.dev` is a Bun workspace monorepo with a Next.js frontend in `frontend/` and a Sanity Studio in `studio/`. The Studio reads and writes content in Sanity's hosted Content Lake; the frontend renders published pages and articles. How each feature works is documented in `docs/`. Read the relevant page before changing a feature and update it with the change.

## Before you write code

- Next.js 16 may differ from what you know. Read the relevant guide in `frontend/node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before changing frontend code.
- For Sanity schemas, queries, or Studio configuration, use the `sanity-best-practices` skill and read the relevant reference.
- Skills live in the repository-level `.agents/skills/`; use Next.js and React skills for `frontend/`, Sanity and content-modeling skills for `studio/`, and both for Sanity integration work in the frontend. Do not edit installed skills by hand.

## Rules

Each rule links to its detail in `.agents/rules/`: what to look for, what not to flag, and an example. Read the detail when a change touches that area, and run the `reviewer` agent (`.agents/experts/reviewer.md`) before opening a pull request.

- Every frontend route stays static: `○`, or `●` for prerendered Sanity pages, in the build output. Route handlers export `dynamic = "force-static"` ([rule](.agents/rules/frontend/static-routes.md)).
- Server Components are the default; add `"use client"` only for real client-side interaction.
- Keep components few. Hardcode content where it is used; extract only for real behaviour or reuse. Frontend imports use `@/*`; shared Studio helpers use `@studio/*` ([rule](.agents/rules/frontend/server-components-default.md)).
- Global styles are imported only through `frontend/src/app/layout.tsx`. Use the `background` and `foreground` colours, never hardcoded black or white, and keep text contrast at 4.5:1 or more in both schemes ([rule](.agents/rules/frontend/color-scheme-contrast.md)).
- The homepage has one `<h1>`; page sections use `<h2>` and entries `<h3>`. Never skip heading ranks or add deeper headings without real subsections ([rule](.agents/rules/frontend/heading-ranks.md)).
- Keep landmarks, accessible names, visible focus styles, semantic lists, native links, and the reduced-motion rules in `frontend/src/features/style/animations.css` ([rule](.agents/rules/frontend/landmarks-and-focus.md)).
- Sanity pages and articles are separate document types with direct Portable Text bodies. Keep their routes, body content, and metadata aligned ([rule](.agents/rules/content/pages-vs-posts.md)).
- Profile details are repeated in Sanity content (pages and the `settings` document), `frontend/src/features/pages/metadata.ts`, `frontend/src/app/llms.txt/route.ts`, and `frontend/src/app/manifest.ts`. Change them together ([rule](.agents/rules/content/profile-details-sync.md)).
- Next.js merges `openGraph` shallowly, so declare the complete object ([rule](.agents/rules/frontend/opengraph-complete.md)).
- `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, `llms.txt`, and `feed.xml` list only canonical public content; `/api/` stays disallowed, and the 404 stays `noindex` ([rule](.agents/rules/content/canonical-discovery.md)).

## Quality

Run `bun run qa` from the repository root before considering work complete. Run `bun run format` before committing. Verify UI changes in a real browser with `bunx agent-browser` ([browser automation](docs/frontend/browser-automation.md)).

Tests use Vitest, live next to the frontend code as `*.test.ts(x)`, and import their APIs from `vitest`. Test only real logic that is easy to break, not hardcoded content or framework behaviour. Do not add a separate test workspace ([rule](.agents/rules/repo/tests-real-logic.md)).

## Conventions

- Write everything in English: commits, PRs, file names, code, UI copy, and documentation. Use kebab-case file names.
- Commits follow Zed's style: `area: Description`, imperative and sentence case, such as `bios: Use ui-sdk shortcuts for reboot`. Omit the area only for project-wide changes. No trailing period and no Co-authored-by trailers.
- `main` is the only long-lived branch. Work on a short kebab-case branch named after its area and squash-merge it through a pull request that follows `.github/pull_request_template.md` ([rule](.agents/rules/repo/commits-and-prs.md)).
- No explanatory comments in code; put lasting decisions in `docs/` ([rule](.agents/rules/repo/docs-follow-code.md)).
- Frontend environment values go in `frontend/.env.local`; document them in `frontend/.env.example`.
- The MIT License covers code only; keep its personal-content exclusion.

## Rules hygiene

Rules are traps to avoid, not maps to follow. The stack and folder layout are in the [README](README.md); how features work is in `docs/`.

- Do not edit `.rules` or `.agents/rules/` during normal feature or fix work. If you hit a trap a rule would have prevented, propose the rule in the pull request description instead.
- A rule is one bullet here and one file in `.agents/rules/` in the [rule format](.agents/README.md#rule-format). Add, change, or remove both together.
- Add a rule only when an agent made, or would plausibly make, the same mistake again. One incident in unusual code is not enough.
- Do not put here what the code, the docs, or the tooling already enforce: project structure, feature explanations, formatting, or lint rules.
- When a rule no longer applies, remove it in the same pull request that changes the code.

